// Build-A-Player College Football, All-Time: each school's best one to three players ever at QB, at RB and at WR.
// The All-Time pool of the College Football mode (components/cfb/CFBMode.jsx: the Current / All-Time switch on its
// first screen; loaded through lib/cfbLoad.js loadCFBLegends, only when a build is All-Time).
// Written by the legends build (hand lists + checks, 2026-11-10). Edit a rating here by hand if you must; the
// counts and the notes below are as of that build.
//
// ── Who is here ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1078 (QB 373, RB 352, WR 353) legends. A school sits in its 2026 conference (data/cfb-teams.js), so the wheel is the same
// eleven conferences the current pool spins. Three where a school has three greats at the spot, fewer where it
// doesn't, never none: every school has at least one legend at QB, at RB and at WR (a newer FBS program's is from
// its FCS or small-college years: Texas State's Johnny Parker, Missouri State's John Gianini and Jermaine Saffold):
//   SEC           QB 46 · RB 44 · WR 46
//   Big Ten       QB 53 · RB 52 · WR 51
//   Big 12        QB 47 · RB 45 · WR 44
//   ACC           QB 51 · RB 48 · WR 51
//   Pac-12        QB 20 · RB 22 · WR 21
//   Mountain West QB 23 · RB 24 · WR 24
//   American      QB 33 · RB 31 · WR 27
//   Sun Belt      QB 35 · RB 31 · WR 32
//   C-USA         QB 27 · RB 18 · WR 19
//   MAC           QB 33 · RB 31 · WR 33
//   Independents  QB  5 · RB  6 · WR  5
// COLLEGE legends: picked and graded on what they did in college (Heisman winners and finalists, All-Americans,
// national champions, the school's record holders), not on their NFL careers. Stanford's quarterbacks are Luck,
// Plunkett and Elway; Mississippi State's is Dak Prescott alone. A halfback or tailback of the single-wing years is
// a running back here (Kinnick, Harmon, Doak Walker), an end is a receiver (Bill Carpenter, Buddy Dial). A player
// can be on two schools (Russell Wilson: NC State and Wisconsin; Diego Pavia: New Mexico State and Vanderbilt).
//
// ── The row (the same shape as data/cfb-players.js, so the same code reads both) ──────────────────────────────
// starter: true on every row (the wheel deals starters; all of a school's legends are dealt). depth: 1-3, the
//   order they're listed in, the school's greatest first.
// years: the seasons he played at the school ("2006–09"), where a current player has his class. Seasons played,
//   not years enrolled: a redshirt or sit-out year isn't counted (Baker Mayfield at Oklahoma is 2015–17).
// height (in) / weight (lb): height from Wikipedia's infobox where it has one (the measured height), else the
//   listed height; weight is the college playing weight, not a later NFL one.
// number: his college number where known (not shown anywhere in the mode at QB, RB or WR).
// espn / headshot: the ESPN athlete id his record was matched on (his NFL page's id where he has one, else his
//   college page's) and his photo, see Headshots below. A row without headshot shows initials.
// skin: the legends files' five tones (the figure's skin when his chip is on the build).
//
// ── Grades (0-11: F D C- C C+ B- B B+ A- A A+ S, the college scale of data/cfb-players.js) ────────────────────
// Hand-graded on his college seasons, trait by trait: an option quarterback gets legs, not arm (Frazier, Crouch,
// Dowis); a pocket passer gets no legs (Marino, Wuerffel); a 5'8" back gets no Size. Size is never graded by hand:
// it is the current file's own formula on his height and weight (QB -88.30 + 1.0211 x in + 0.08298 x lb; WR 75%
// height + 25% weight; RB 80% weight + 20% height), so a legend and a current player of the same build get the
// same Size. ovr: the game's own formulas (utils/simulation.js calcOVR, calcOVRRB, calcOVRWR).
// Calibrated against the current pool as it stood on 2026-11-10, after its one-grade lift
// (current starters, OVR p10 / median / p90 / max: QB 79/86/94/98 · RB 79/84/90/93 · WR 80/85/92/99):
//   1. The hand grades were made against the earlier pool and already sat a tier above it. When the pool went up a
//      grade, the legends' lower grades went up with it: QB B+ and below, WR B and below, RB B- and below, one step.
//      (One step on everything, as the current pool got, put the median legend at 93-95 with over a hundred at 99.)
//   2. The top end by hand: about 290 legends were placed by résumé (99 for the Heisman winners and the players
//      who owned a season: Tebow, Vince Young, Barry Sanders, Herschel Walker, Fitzgerald, Moss; 97-98 for the
//      finalists and the multi-year All-Americans; and a few mid-major stat lines pulled back down), their grades
//      moved a step at a time to land there, weak traits left weak and signature traits left alone.
//   3. No floor (the owner's rule): a legend is graded on his own college résumé, even where that leaves a school's
//      best legend below the man starting there now (16 schools/positions today, FAU's Rusty Smith behind its
//      current quarterback among them). Nobody is raised to stay ahead of a current player.
//   Result: QB 87/91/97/99 · RB 90/93/98/99 · WR 88/91/96/99.
//
// ── Checks (2026-11-10) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every row was checked for the school and the position against at least one of: the player's Wikipedia infobox
// (college, position: facts only, nothing copied), ESPN's athlete record (college, position, draft year), and the
// school's "football statistical leaders" page (the record book: is he in it, and for which seasons). The years
// were checked two ways: inside the years Wikipedia has him at the school, and against the seasons the record book
// lists him for; 31 rows were corrected by hand where they disagreed, and one player was replaced (Akron's career
// rushing leader is Mike Clark, 1984–86). Not checked against any source: jersey numbers (from memory, left out
// where not sure), playing weights (listed college weights from memory), and the height and weight of Mike Clark
// (Akron), Johnny Parker (Texas State) and John Gianini (Missouri State: his 240 lb is the school's, his height is
// not), which are estimates. Eddie Conti (Delaware) is the one row none of the three sources covers.
// The three small-college additions were checked against the schools themselves: Johnny Parker, wide receiver
// 1969–71, career leader in catches (160) and yards (2,479) (Texas State Hall of Honor, 2016); John Gianini,
// fullback 1976–80, the career rushing leader, who led the Bears in rushing four seasons (MSU Athletics Hall of
// Fame, 1997); Jermaine Saffold, wide receiver 2009–11, the career receiving-yards leader with 2,221
// (missouristatebears.com; ESPN's record has him a Missouri State WR).
//
// ── Headshots (ESPN only) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 595 rows have one. ESPN's own player photo, fetched the way scripts/fetch-cfb-assets.mjs and
// scripts/fetch-lb-legend-headshots.mjs do it (ESPN's combiner, 350x254 crop, webp q75), every one looked at:
//   headshot "cfb_{id}"  = his ESPN college-football photo (public/headshots/cfb_{id}.webp)
//   headshot "espn_{id}" = his ESPN NFL player photo, in an NFL jersey (public/headshots/espn_{id}.webp)
// No Wikipedia, no Hall of Fame, sideline or action shots. ESPN has no photo for almost anyone who was done playing
// before about 2005, and for most players who never reached an NFL roster before about 2015: those show initials.
// Left out on purpose (initials): ESPN's photo is the coach he is now, not the player (Jim Harbaugh, Eric Bieniemy,
// Kellen Moore, Henry Ellard, Collin Klein; DeSean Jackson's college photo is too, so his is the NFL one), or the
// photo under his id doesn't look like his (Kevin Smith, UCF; Craig Heyward, Pitt). A father and a son at the same
// school share a name on ESPN: Peter Warrick, Willie Taggart, Eddie Lee Ivery and Deland McCullough's photos are
// the sons', so the fathers have none. Jordan Matthews' is his own NFL page, not the Vanderbilt cornerback's.

